  "content": {
    "id": "p26-q11-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "11",
    "prompt": "First use the formula $S_n=\\frac{n}{2}(2a+(n-1)d)$ to find the sum $S_n$ for each arithmetic series.\n\nThen use quadratic equations to find the number of terms if $S_n$ has the given value.",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p26-q11-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$42+40+38+\\cdots$ where $S_n=0$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$S_n=n(43-n)$; $43$ terms.",
          "worked": "$a=42$, $d=-2$.\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}S_n&=\\frac{n}{2}\\bigl(2(42)+(n-1)(-2)\\bigr)\\\\S_n&=\\frac{n}{2}(84-2n+2)\\\\S_n&=\\frac{n}{2}(86-2n)\\\\S_n&=n(43-n)\\end{aligned}$$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}S_n&=0\\\\n(43-n)&=0\\\\n&=0\\text{ or }43\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSince $n>0$, there are $43$ terms.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 60
      },
      {
        "id": "p26-q11-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$60+57+54+\\cdots$ where $S_n=0$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$S_n=\\frac{3n}{2}(41-n)$; $41$ terms.",
          "worked": "$a=60$, $d=-3$.\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}S_n&=\\frac{n}{2}\\bigl(2(60)+(n-1)(-3)\\bigr)\\\\S_n&=\\frac{n}{2}(120-3n+3)\\\\S_n&=\\frac{n}{2}(123-3n)\\\\S_n&=\\frac{3n}{2}(41-n)\\end{aligned}$$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}S_n&=0\\\\\\frac{3n}{2}(41-n)&=0\\\\n&=0\\text{ or }41\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSince $n>0$, there are $41$ terms.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 63
      },
      {
        "id": "p26-q11-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$45+51+57+\\cdots$ where $S_n=153$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$S_n=3n(14+n)$; $3$ terms.",
          "worked": "$a=45$, $d=6$.\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}S_n&=\\frac{n}{2}\\bigl(2(45)+(n-1)(6)\\bigr)\\\\S_n&=\\frac{n}{2}(90+6n-6)\\\\S_n&=\\frac{n}{2}(84+6n)\\\\S_n&=3n(14+n)\\end{aligned}$$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}S_n&=153\\\\3n(14+n)&=153\\\\3n^2+42n&=153\\\\n^2+14n-51&=0\\\\(n-3)(n+17)&=0\\\\n&=3\\text{ or }-17\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSince $n>0$, there are $3$ terms.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 76
      },
      {
        "id": "p26-q11-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "$2\\frac12+3+3\\frac12+\\cdots$ where $S_n=22\\frac12$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$S_n=\\frac{n}{4}(9+n)$; $6$ terms.",
          "worked": "$a=\\frac52$, $d=\\frac12$.\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}S_n&=\\frac{n}{2}\\left(2\\left(\\frac52\\right)+(n-1)\\left(\\frac12\\right)\\right)\\\\S_n&=\\frac{n}{2}\\left(5+\\frac n2-\\frac12\\right)\\\\S_n&=\\frac{n}{2}\\left(\\frac92+\\frac n2\\right)\\\\S_n&=\\frac n4(9+n)\\end{aligned}$$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}S_n&=22\\frac12\\\\\\frac n4(9+n)&=\\frac{45}{2}\\\\n(9+n)&=90\\\\n^2+9n-90&=0\\\\(n-6)(n+15)&=0\\\\n&=6\\text{ or }-15\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSince $n>0$, there are $6$ terms.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 83
      }
    ]
  },
